{
  "result": "There is a specific kind of silence that only exists at 5:47 in the morning. It isn't the silence of night, which is heavy and complete, nor the silence of an empty room. It's a thin, expectant silence, as if the world is holding its breath before remembering what it was supposed to do today.\n\nI like to be awake for it. I like making coffee while the house is cool, watching the steam curl against the window where the first light is just starting to smudge the dark blue into grey. The refrigerator hums. A bird tests a single note outside, then decides it's too early and stops.\n\nMornings are honest in a way no other time is. You haven't yet put on your personality for the day. You haven't checked your phone and borrowed other people's anxieties. You are just you, sleep-soft and rumpled, standing in socks in the kitchen.\n\nWe treat mornings as a starting line, a place to be productive, to optimize, to conquer. But I think they're more like a doorway. You don't have to sprint through a doorway. You can stand in it for a moment and feel both rooms at once \u2014 the night you just left and the day you haven't entered yet.\n\nThat in-between is where most good ideas live. Not in the rush, but in the pause before it. If you listen closely, that 5:47 silence isn't empty at all. It's full of possibility, humming, waiting for you to answer.",
